<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tumu Crisis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1449">1449</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Tumu Fortress</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tumu Crisis was the defeat and capture of the Ming emperor Zhengtong by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirat</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under Esen at Tumu fortress, north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in September 1449. The emperor had led a large army against the Oirat under the influence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-zhen-eunuch">Wang Zhen</a>, who was killed. The Ming court enthroned his brother as the Jingtai Emperor and held Beijing, and Esen released the captive emperor in 1450.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tumu-crisis.jpg" alt="Tumu Crisis" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tumu_Crisis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ming Great Wall seen by visitors today is largely a 16th-century brick-faced work, built after the Zhengtong Emperor was captured by the Oirats at <b>Tumu</b> in 1449.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Toghon and his son Esen built Oirat power in the 15th century; Esen defeated and captured the Ming emperor Zhengtong at <b>Tumu</b> in 1449 and briefly took the title of khan himself before being killed in 1455.</blockquote><a href="/state/four-oirat">Four Oirat</a></li><li><blockquote>After a Ming victory at Lake Buir in 1388, power fragmented, and the Four Oirat often dominated; their leader Esen captured the Ming emperor at <b>Tumu</b> in 1449.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-yuan">Northern Yuan</a></li></ul></section>
